---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href, lessonPath, playgroundPath, PLAYGROUNDS } from '../../site';

const pg = PLAYGROUNDS.find((p) => p.slug === 'search-vs-construction')!;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base title={pg.title} description={pg.summary} current={playgroundPath(pg.slug)} wide>
  <p class="eyebrow">Playground</p>
  <h1>{pg.title}</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Pick an order n. The search below tries to build every (v, k, 1) difference set with v = n² + n + 1 points and
    k = n + 1 members, one member at a time, backing up whenever a difference repeats. It runs in a background
    thread, so the page stays responsive, and each set it finds is checked against the Singer sets for the same
    order.
  </p>
  <p>
    The search only looks for sets that contain 0 and 1. That loses nothing: the difference 1 occurs exactly once, as
    a − b = 1 for one pair, and sliding the set back by b gives a copy that contains 0 and 1. So each family of
    translates shows up exactly once. The <em>completeness conjecture</em> of <a
      href={href(base, lessonPath('17-every-planar-set-singer'))}>Lesson 17</a> predicts that every set found is of
    Singer type: a Singer set up to sliding, or a mirror image of one.
  </p>
  <div data-widget="planar-search" data-n="7" data-url-state data-autorun></div>
  <h2>What to try</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>n = 6</strong> finishes at once with nothing found. The Bruck–Ryser theorem already says so: 6 leaves
      remainder 2 when divided by 4 and is not a sum of two squares.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>n = 10</strong> passes Bruck–Ryser (10 = 1² + 3²), and the search rules it out by brute force in about a
      second. Ruling out <em>every</em> plane of order 10, cyclic or not, took Lam, Thiel, and Swiercz years of
      computer time (1989): a cyclic plane is far more constrained.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>n = 11 and 12</strong> take seconds to minutes, and the work grows about ninefold with each step in n.
      Singer's construction, in the last box, stays far below a millisecond: it writes the set down instead of
      searching for it.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
